WEBVTT

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:02.590
He's here.

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.120
Wonderful. I've been counting the minutes, Marilyn.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:05.960
Who's here?

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:07.470
Morning, Joel.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.950
Holland, hey, you look terrible.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.870
Have a seat. Let's take a peek under the hood.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:14.920
What's this?

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.090
The IRS.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:19.130
Read it.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:21.770
It has come to our attention.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:25.270
Our investigators have determined you owe the government $9,000?

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:26.960
Keep going.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.960
Holland, it says here you haven't paid your taxes since 19...

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.390
1959 correct 1959 statehood i voted against it what you've been withholding taxes as a form of

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:42.310
political protest well not exactly i figured they'd they'd let me know if they really needed

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:49.190
it you never filed a tax return no i mean i've been delinquent once or twice myself but um

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:54.880
i don't think you can get a 32-year extension well i figured something more like a note a note

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.980
from you what you mean like a medical excuse there you go you want

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.340
me to write to the Internal Revenue Service saying you can't pay your taxes because you

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:04.100
aren't feeling well?

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.620
Well, don't get me wrong.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.600
I fully intend to pay it, but not right now.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.920
It'll take me a little while to get up that kind of cash.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.240
What'd he say?

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.820
Good morning, Sicily. It's 7.02, a soggy 34 degrees outside.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.260
It's time to reach into the K-Bear mailbag.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.800
Dave from Mosquito Lake writes,

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.520
Dear Chris in the morning, I loved your series on Islam. How about a transcript?

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.840
Well, sorry, Dave, there isn't one. I work without a net, you know?

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:19.590
I'm glad you like the series.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:24.130
On a personal note, my brother Bernard drops a note from the Dark Continent.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.220
Dear Chris, Africa is all I dreamed it would be.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:29.980
I trekked up the Usama...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.280
range, that's in Tanzania folks, where I watched the sunrise and felt the blood of

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:43.510
my ancestors pulse through my veins. Best to all, Quahiri, Bernard. Well that's

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:47.430
something, huh? Bernard's out there in the motherland hugging the curves down

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:53.330
life's highways and yours truly creeps along here at his petty pace day to day

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:58.960
full of sound and fury, lots of unnecessary commercial messages.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.120
Truth is, folks, I've been dragging my psychic feet lately.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.960
Probably nothing more than your usual biorhythmic peaks and valleys,

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:15.300
but there's no getting around the fact that we all need a pump, you know?

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.060
Something to make us jump up out of bed in the morning

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.760
and greet the dawn with a big old cosmic howdy.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.240
Wait, wait, wait. Hold it right there. What is that?

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.640
This is Tex Quarantine, and this is a 7-grain waffle with gooseberry syrup.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.380
Hey, Dr. Whitman.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.280
Don't talk with your mouth full, Ed.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.370
That appears to be cheese blitzes with blueberry sauce.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:48.100
He's back.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.680
No, you can't drown it.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:56.900
It's got to float.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.100
It's got to breathe.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:59.980
Look, look, you're not ready for crazy.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.500
Just go boil some water or something, please.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:02.800
Hey, Adam.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:04.090
Ah, okay.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:05.390
That's it.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:06.480
I'm out of here.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.800
Wait, wait, what did I say?

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:08.680
What did you say?

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.180
Yeah, I came into...

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:10.540
To what?

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:11.880
To idly interrupt?

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.900
To annoy beyond reason?

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:15.640
No, I thought that maybe...

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.080
I suppose you let uninvited guests barge into your operating room all the time, huh?

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:21.000
Mushrooms.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:21.840
I love shiitoshis.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.000
Those are chanterelles, you moron.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:24.260
Hey!

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.190
Do you want to die?

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:27.380
Okay, okay.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.500
So, uh, what brings you back to Sicily?

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.230
You're right. Let's try an easier one.

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:32.930
Um, how's Eve?

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:34.410
She's sick.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:35.690
Oh, what a surprise.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.910
Hey, just watch it, okay?

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:39.760
She doesn't want to see me, does she?

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.820
Why, do you have a problem with that, doctor?

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:43.300
Dave, Dave.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:51.900
Patient.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.620
No, Eve's sciatic nerve was acting up again.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:03.790
And then before you could say booze, she comes down with a case of thyroiditis.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.020
Did she try a vitamin B supplement?

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.230
What a brilliant idea.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:11.430
Yeah, of course we did, with amazing results.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:12.590
Nothing happened.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.600
So now she's at Dr. Chang's clinic.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:18.460
Dr. Chang?

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:20.530
Dr. M. Chang?

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.960
Well, I'm glad to see you keep up with the literature, Fleischman.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:29.980
I mean, he's only the seminal force in the field of acupuncture, and spare me the A.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.140
party line, okay? I know all about it.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.260
If it isn't in the New England Journal of Medicine,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.840
it never happened for you guys, right?

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:37.640
I was just gonna ask where he practices.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:39.680
Hunan, if you must know.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.160
Hunan is in China?

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.880
No, Hunan is in North Dakota.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:45.540
So Eve is in China.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:47.900
Talking to myself?

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.320
Yes, Eve is in China.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:51.960
I wish I was there with her.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:54.260
I could be translating cookbooks instead of being

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.420
interrogated by some gastronomic pygmy.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:57.400
You read Chinese?

00:05:58.560 --> 00:05:59.680
Cantonese, like a native.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.380
Mandarin still gives me a fit.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:02.960
Come on.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:06.340
Chorn pong, but you doom pong.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:08.160
What does that mean?

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:09.600
Loosely translated.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:14.280
It means quick and painless or slow and lingering.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:21.150
Hold it.

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.260
What the hell do you think you're doing?

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:23.800
Me?

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:24.660
Yeah.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:25.900
What have you got in your hand there?

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:27.050
Newspaper.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:28.920
It's not just a newspaper.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:29.800
That's my newspaper.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.270
The Sicily News and World Telegram.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:33.350
Sorry.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:36.350
Where I come from, people respect their local newspapers.

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:39.980
They read them, they clip stories, they discuss them.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.640
They do not use them to clean up after their pets.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:44.450
Do I make myself clear?

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:45.430
Yeah, but...

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:46.910
You want clean sidewalks?

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:48.110
Here.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:56.660
Flashman, you're gonna write you that note, hon?

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.170
It says it's creaky.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:06:59.970
I suppose it's right.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.740
Don't worry. We'll come up with the money.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:03.510
How?

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:07.900
I don't know. I was just saying it to be nice.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.440
I just hate seeing you drag that cute nose on the floor, babe.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.360
Anyway, what's the worst thing that can happen?

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:20.660
Well, they could seize the bar, sell the property, put me in jail.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:35.420
All right, listen up, all of you.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.490
Hey, yeah, you.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.960
Something's been festering under the skin of this town like an angry boil,

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:44.580
and I think it's time we gave it a scratch.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:46.340
How about a clue, Maurice?

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:51.560
Case in point, Ruthann, what is that you're reading, dear?

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.320
The Christian Science Monitor.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:55.080
Uh-huh.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:59.980
Well, you know we have a paper in this town, but...

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.040
Rather than support our local news organ,

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:07.690
some people would prefer to use it as a sponge to mop up spill decaf.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:09.620
You've got a problem here, people.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:13.300
The problem, Maurice, is that your paper is a major snooze.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.880
You think the New York Times is a joyride?

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:19.490
You think people read the New York Times because they like to?

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.280
No, no. People read the New York Times because they feel like they ought to.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:25.540
Isn't that right, Fleischman?

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.050
Oh, foolie. There's nothing to write about around here, and you know what.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:29.970
Oh, you think...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.430
things are too uneventful here in the borough of arrowhead county huh well yes i do well let me tell

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:41.900
you about a little item in the new york times today it's about the village sentinel in duxbury

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:50.570
vermont a thriving metropolis of 900 souls they have been nominated for a pulitzer prize why

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:59.730
ticks my paper has ticks my paper has bugs of all kinds

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:06.120
Show of hands, how many of you read the article on mosquito abatement program in Loon Lake?

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:10.900
See, it's just my point.

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:16.880
A great newspaper needs a great reading public.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:23.140
We've all got to pull our own weight here, people.

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:27.460
End of story.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:32.830
Hey there, Chris.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:34.230
Hey, Holling, come on in.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.200
Another postcard from Bernard?

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:42.200
Yeah, he's in Sierra Leone now, northwest coast, below Guinea.

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:43.750
That's an adventure.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:45.920
Got a rash, nothing serious.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:49.500
Maybe this is not a good time.

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:51.940
No, no, no, I'm sorry, Holling.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.080
What's up, buddy?

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:54.060
Well, not too much.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:57.360
I owe Uncle Sam $9,000.

00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:59.410
Ooh, back taxes?

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.380
I've never asked anyone for cash before, Chris,

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:04.560
but those government boys can be rough.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:05.540
How much you need?

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:07.560
Well, it's no secret with your inheritance

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.360
you're the only fellow around who's liquid,

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:10.480
other than Maurice.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:12.860
Now, there was a time when I could ask him.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.480
No, no, no, full amount, 5% interest on it.

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:17.080
No, no, hold on a minute.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:18.360
I'm not talking about a loan.

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:19.550
You not?

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:21.550
No, I don't believe in debt.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:23.680
What a man can't pay for, he can't own.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:25.950
No, this is a business investment.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:29.430
One half interest in the brick for $9,000.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:32.170
Me? Own half of your bar?

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:33.410
Well, it's a good offer.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:35.690
I had the bar stools upholstered last year.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:36.930
Oh, man. Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa,

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.450
Holling, Holling, you and me, partners in the brick.

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:40.150
Is that what you're saying?

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:42.830
Well, yeah. Oh, man.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:44.960
Holling, this was meant to be.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:45.900
This was meant to happen.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:48.960
Holling, ever since I inherited that money,

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:52.780
my karma has been all dressed up with nowhere to go, man.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.000
I love bars. You know that.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.540
Holling, I dream about bars.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:58.010
Best talk I ever had with my dad in a bar, man.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:59.270
I patched him up after a fight.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:10:59.970
Holling.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:01.820
I know the layout of every bar I've ever been in.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:03.580
I know the bartender's name.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.100
I know what they charge me for a drink.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:06.160
I was made for this, Holling.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.240
I was made to own a bar.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.480
I take it you're interested.

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:16.710
Coming on down.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:19.880
All right, next, huh, Joel?

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:21.600
Nothing for me, Chris.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:23.040
Come on now.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:24.520
Maggie Ruthanne on the house.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:25.460
Why not?

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:27.140
Hey, that okay with you, huh?

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:28.590
It's your bar, partner.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:29.980
Hey, this...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.740
This is interesting. We're 14% ahead of last year's precipitation count.

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:35.210
Doggy!

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.390
Uh, here's a little something I'm trying with, uh, polenta.

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:43.240
Fabulous. Fabulous.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.620
Delicious. What is this? This is, um, cilantro.

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:49.810
Why? You want to try this at home?

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:53.350
I devote my life to creating culinary masterpieces.

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:55.450
Do you think I'd reduce that to a shopping list?

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:56.600
I just...

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:59.520
No, I suppose every passenger thinks they can fly your plane, huh?

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.320
What's that, hot libs? A little left rudder, slide over.

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:06.080
But any jerk with a hot plate can cook, right?

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:07.960
A little cilantro, you're a chef.

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:10.370
What's this, Ed?

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:13.820
Well, I thought about what you said, Maurice, about initiative.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:15.480
This is my new column.

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:17.190
Ed goes to the movies?

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:21.010
Yeah. Four bear claws means instant classic.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:24.030
And one bear claw means stay home and read.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.060
I think it's time people knew the truth.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:27.720
Oh, Chelly!

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:29.740
Mmm, what are these?

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:31.100
I don't know what you call them,

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:32.840
but I've been scarfing them down like beer nuts.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.650
Oh, my God.

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:37.630
This is amazing.

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:39.050
What are these?

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:41.930
Diego's Mez Azul Supreme.

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:42.980
Diego?

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:43.900
A friend.

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:46.150
Had a way with blue cornflower.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:49.320
Yeah, he would have been one of the greats if only...

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:50.020
If only what?

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:52.640
If only he hadn't been blown to bits

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:54.880
along with his deux chavots outside of Lima.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:56.560
Faulty detonator.

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:59.970
Yeah, give that guy a feature.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.800
Charcoal briquettes, some hickory chips, and a goat.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:04.470
He'd work miracles.

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:07.450
Too bad. Nice kid.

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:08.990
Should have been me.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:10.300
Wow.

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:11.910
Lima, Peru?

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:14.010
Yeah, well, the outskirts of the city, actually.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:15.510
What were you doing in Peru?

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:17.250
You think I'm going to tell you, lady?

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:18.770
Hey, folks, come on.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:20.330
I mean, his food is great.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:23.210
It's better than great, but this is Adam we're talking about.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:25.290
The man is a walking pathology.

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:27.190
He cannot tell the time without lying.

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:29.790
I mean, one can only guess at the unique circumstance.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.060
that rendered him incapable of distinguishing Rocky and Bo Winkle from the evening news.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:36.280
Don't you think you're being a little harsh, Joel?

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:40.100
No, no, no. Please, please continue.

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:44.860
I'm sure everyone here is fascinated to hear more pop psychology

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:47.760
from number 54 in his class at Columbia Mid.

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:53.300
54th? Well, out of 140, it happened to be a very tough school, O'Connell.

00:13:53.420 --> 00:13:54.040
How did you know that?

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:59.880
What are you staring at? Good room lucky.

